{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# Defect attribution (Stage 1) — TWO origins, plus one same-day aggravator\n\nBlamed the implicated LINES on `origin/main` (my checkout was stale; `origin/main` line numbers\nmatch the RCA exactly, including the §2d block at `:291-292`). Walked every blame hit back through\nits carriers with `git blame -w -C -M` + `git log -S`.\n\n## ORIGIN A — the latch: `786b-190d-5e3a-4194` (`smelly-pane-bolt`)\n\nCommit `6cd6f4e1f7c0` (2026-06-29, \"S2 (786b): deploy Gerrit + review-bot receiver behind\nnginx/TLS\"). Pre-trailer commit; the ticket is named in the subject and independently corroborated\n— bug `4d8c-c1f9-5ea4-454d` already records `caused_by 786b-190d-5e3a-4194` for this same SHA.\n\nIt created `install-observability.sh` with BOTH halves of the latch in one hunk: `Type=oneshot`\nwith no `TimeoutStartSec` (`:20-27`) and `OnUnitActiveSec=5min` (`:31`). Unchanged since — no\ncarriers, and a whole-file blame would say the same thing. This half of the defect was wrong from\ncreation and is independent of what the script does: any hang, from any command, latches the timer\noff permanently.\n\n## ORIGIN B — the scan cost: `34cd-1a7c-b9b1-4b05` (`equestrian-clueless-cowbird`)\n\nCommit `e9d4d390c364` (2026-08-03, \"34cd: defer autodeploy while a review runs\"). Line `:883` — the\nscan inside `publish_autodeploy_marker_delta` — blames straight here and is NOT a carrier: the line\nis byte-identical to how 34cd introduced it.\n\n34cd is the origin because it changed the SHAPE of the cost. Before it, a journal scan was per LOG\nSOURCE (a fixed handful). 34cd put a complete unbounded whole-journal scan inside a reusable\n**per-marker** helper, making cost linear in the number of published markers. The call sites then\ngrew as designed, by commits that are amplifiers rather than defects:\n\n```\n3  e9d4d390c364  2026-08-03  34cd: defer autodeploy while a review runs   <- helper created\n5  5a3ad479f0c6  2026-08-12  Split review-interrupt metric by reason\n6  5961cefff435  2026-08-21  Publish disk-pressure prunes as a metric\n8  2a0a4b19f2ab  2026-08-25  infra: add mcp blue-green autodeploy target\n9  6546114de21a  2026-08-28  Reclaim tagged mcp images and prove it\n```\n\nOn `origin/main` today that is 9 helper calls + 4 fixed scans = **13** whole-journal scans per run\n(the RCA counted 12 against a slightly older tree; the direction is the same).\n\n## Carriers I walked through and REJECTED — where I disagree with a naive blame\n\nA whole-file blame, and even a per-line `git blame` on `:760/:788/:816`, reports\n**`43db7785256e` (2026-08-12, ticket `8c2f-8377-5044-4650`, `colossal-slothful-grouse`)**. That is\nWRONG. 8c2f only changed the grep PATTERN (unanchored substring -> `^TOKEN \\{` record anchor); it\nrewrote the whole line, which is why `-w -C -M` still lands on it. `git log -S` gives the real\nintroducers: `1d1a072a75c7` (2026-06-29, S4b/918c, voter), `46acd6e2507c` (merge-change),\n`49ad69bcd242` (2026-07-01, epic 1fa8, g2p), `479c3a57b4c0` (2026-07-03, `8903-efb2-b73f-4e22`,\nautodeploy unit). Those four ARE the first unbounded scans — but by the date test they are not the\ncause of a 940 s overrun: at their dates the box was days old, the journal small, and there were\none to four scans. They became dangerous only once 34cd made the count grow without bound.\n\n## The \"unmet obligation\" hypothesis — TESTED and REJECTED as the origin\n\nI checked whether the origin is the change that CREATED the §2d bounding obligation and failed to\napply it to the journal scans:\n\n- `972fa4dd932d` (2026-09-03, ticket `9ea3-7d07-ea55-4496`) first bounded docker calls in this\n  script with `timeout 15`, reasoning \"the docker calls here are the ones that can WEDGE THE\n  5-MINUTE TIMER\".\n- `bbe5d228bd2f` (2026-09-04, ticket `9183-aaae-667d-45e6`, `geode-joking-cockatiel`) added\n  `DOCKER_DU_TIMEOUT`/`DOCKER_DF_TIMEOUT` at `:291-292` and named it \"the §2d rule\".\n\n**Date test says no.** The wrong behaviour — 13 unbounded whole-journal scans in a 300 s probe over\na journal permitted to reach 4 GiB, which the RCA measures as exhausting the period at ~1.37 GB —\nexisted from 2026-08-03, a month before the rule was written. These two changes are a **missed\ngeneralisation / last clear chance**, not the cause. They matter because they are what makes the\nRCA's \"violates its own documented contract\" argument available, and they make the repair\nobviously in scope — but attributing the outage to them would be backwards.\n\n(The repo-wide obligation in `AGENTS.md` §\"Bound background helpers at spawn\" also postdates 34cd:\nearliest is `43f783de2a2d`, 2026-08-27.)\n\n## SAME-DAY AGGRAVATOR, offered as a hypothesis and NOT as a finding\n\n`bbe5d228bd2f` landed **2026-09-04 07:54 PDT, ~6.5 hours before onset at ~14:30 PDT**, and\nautodeploy makes the box reflect `main` on a ~2-minute tick, so it was almost certainly running.\nIt added two UNCONDITIONAL `du -sx` walks per probe run (`:365-366`, over `/var/lib/docker` and\n`/var/lib/docker/overlay2` — measured at the 09-02 incident as 17 GB across 67 overlay2 layer\ndirectories). A stat walk of that size is dentry/inode cache pressure on the same root volume, and\nH1's confirmed account needs exactly one transient allocation at ~14:26 to evict the page cache\nthat had been keeping the probe cheap (8,333 reads / 5 min = ~28 IOPS in the 14:05-14:25 lull).\n\nI am NOT claiming this is the ignition. H1 was evidenced, not experimentally isolated, and I did\nnot reproduce this. It is a coincidence in time with a plausible mechanism, recorded so it is not\nlost. It does not change the attribution: even with a warm cache, 13 unbounded scans over a\n4 GiB-capped journal overrun a 300 s period on their own.\n\n## Summary\n\n| Cause | Ticket | Commit | What it created |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| The latch (permanent silence) | `786b-190d-5e3a-4194` | `6cd6f4e1f7c0` (2026-06-29) | `Type=oneshot` + no `TimeoutStartSec` + `OnUnitActiveSec=5min` |\n| The scan cost (the overrun) | `34cd-1a7c-b9b1-4b05` | `e9d4d390c364` (2026-08-03) | whole-journal scan inside a per-marker helper |\n\nTwo origins, because the symptom genuinely needs both: 34cd supplies a run that overruns the\nperiod, 786b supplies the reason it never came back. Repair follows.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788564375687741400,"uuid":"fbfd754f-4107-4e48-8ade-00ce8f0d5b71"}